seg-9bc8fe53a948 = Bind address

# text
# c1: 127.0.0.1
# c2: ::1
# c3: --http 8080
# c4: 127.0.0.1:8080
seg-82001913f285 = Loopback only (<c1/>, <c2/>). A bare port (<c3/>) means <c4/>.

# text
# c1: --allow-remote
# c2: KSHANA_MCP_HTTP_TOKEN
seg-f2adda2d3e22 = Needs <c1/> <b>and</b> a bearer token in the <c2/> environment variable ({ "16" } characters or more). Without both, the server refuses to start. With both it prints a warning that it is listening beyond this machine.

# text
seg-6fbe07565c36 = Authentication

# text
# c1: Authorization: Bearer <token>
# c2: 401
seg-d5cbeb0cd3d6 = <c1/> on every request when a token is set (it can be set on loopback too). Compared in constant time. Missing or wrong: <c2/>.

# text
seg-1962f434458b = Filesystem

# text
# c1: iq_*
# c2: KSHANA_MCP_IQ_DIR
seg-1a9e048d348d = None. The IQ file tools (<c1/>) are switched off in HTTP mode whatever <c2/> says; every other tool takes its scenario inline and refuses one that names a file. No tool argument reads or writes a path.

# text
seg-19261cdd2ef0 = Body size

# text
# c1: --max-body-bytes
# c2: 413
seg-c4db8c1454f4 = <c1/>, default { "5" } MiB (the scenario cap is { "4" } MiB). Over it: <c2/>, whether the length is declared or the body is chunked.

# text
seg-da9103d627dd = Reply time

# text
# c1: --request-timeout-secs
# c2: 408
# c3: 408
seg-3f013a632773 = <c1/>, default { "120" }. Over it: <c2/>. <b>This bounds how long a caller waits, not the work</b>: a run already under way cannot be stopped, so it carries on after the <c3/> and finishes in the background.

# text
seg-a588b12596b7 = Concurrency

# text
# c1: --max-concurrent
# c2: 503
# c3: Retry-After
seg-aa2214db3bdc = <c1/>, default { "8" }. Beyond it a request gets <c2/> with <c3/> at once; none queues.

# text
seg-87576ded5272 = Work pool

# text
# c1: --max-concurrent
seg-7f9c63e3e5e6 = Every tool call runs on a blocking thread inside a pool of <c1/> slots, never on the async workers, so a long run cannot stop the server answering. A slot is freed only when the run itself ends, not when the caller gives up: abandoned runs therefore cannot pile up past the pool size, and a call that finds the pool full is refused as busy.

# text
seg-4a97b035483c = Work budget

# text
# c1: runs
# c2: kshana
seg-91c788b33b25 = A scenario that asks for far more than the bundled examples is refused before it runs: more than { "1000" } Monte Carlo realisations (<c1/>), a parameter grid of more than { "1000" } nodes, more than { "5000" } member runs (grid nodes times realisations), or a time grid of more than { "2" } million epochs. The error names the field to lower. The same budget applies over stdio; the <c2/> command line has none.

# text
seg-79c6b146637c = CORS

# text
# c1: Access-Control-*
# c2: Origin
# c3: 403
# c4: --allow-origin <scheme://host[:port]>
# c5: *
seg-68fa7d9f1321 = No <c1/> header is ever sent. A request carrying an <c2/> header is refused (<c3/>) unless named with <c4/>; <c5/> is not accepted.

# text
seg-d7f157fdf003 = Host header

# text
# c1: --allowed-host
# c2: --allowed-host your.domain
# c3: 403
seg-d7966e93ef1a = Checked against loopback names, or the bind address plus each <c1/>. Behind a proxy or on a platform URL, pass <c2/>. Anything else: <c3/>.

# text
seg-12d5cf72f363 = Sessions

# text
seg-58dfe5665877 = None. Every POST gets a JSON reply; no session table and no server-sent-event stream is kept open.

# text
# c1: kshana-mcp
seg-adbc92ebc196 = TLS is not terminated by <c1/>. When it is reachable beyond one machine, put HTTPS in front of it (your platform's TLS, or a reverse proxy) and keep the token secret; a bearer token over plain HTTP can be read on the wire.

